{"id": 17, "ts": 1783894026.5186226, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 1, "agent": "agent_3", "message": "I appreciate the clarifications on B and D, and the additional note that C is seen as a \u201cshow\u2011off\u201d and \u201cnot open to new ideas,\u201d which raises concerns about his willingness to collaborate and communicate constructively in the cockpit. While Candidate\u202fA still demonstrates strong operational reliability, quick correct decisions, and a positive crew atmosphere, his difficulty communicating ideas and egocentric attitude remain a risk, especially on long\u2011distance flights where clear, respectful dialogue is critical. Candidate\u202fB matches A on reliability and adds solid stress handling and weather assessment, but his pretentiousness and occasional wrong tone could undermine crew cohesion. Candidate\u202fC shows excellent situational awareness and leadership, yet the \u201cshow\u2011off\u201d perception and lack of openness suggest potential cooperation and communication problems. Candidate\u202fD excels in problem solving and responsibility, but his moody, loner, and \u201cknow\u2011it\u2011all\u201d traits may impair teamwork.\nGiven the current evidence, I remain inclined to provisionally recommend **Candidate\u202fA**, but I am less convinced that his communication shortcomings are fully offset by his reliability. I would like us to compare A\u2019s technical competence and stress resilience directly with B\u2019s, and to consider whether A\u2019s willingness to put others first and his excellent attention skills might mitigate his communication issues."}
